# Portable file and folder names

This team works across macOS and Windows. Git stores whatever path the author's
filesystem accepted, so a name that is fine on macOS can make `git pull` fail for
every Windows colleague with:

```text
error: invalid path 'some/<%= thing %>/file.tsx'
```

There is **no local workaround** for the person on the receiving end. Git validates
path names while building index entries, before sparse-checkout rules are consulted,
so even `git sparse-checkout` cannot skip the offending file. The only fix is a new
commit that renames it — which means the bad name has already blocked everyone.

## Never use in a tracked path

- The characters `< > : " | ? *` or a backslash
- A segment ending in a dot or a space (`build.` / `notes `) — Windows strips these silently
- The reserved Windows device names, with or without an extension: `con`, `prn`,
  `aux`, `nul`, `com0`–`com9`, `lpt0`–`lpt9`
- Two paths that differ only in letter case — they collide on Windows and on a
  default macOS volume

Enforced by `pnpm run check:paths` (`tools/scripts/check-portable-paths.mjs`), which
runs on every PR via `.github/workflows/verify.yml`. It inspects the path strings Git
has recorded, never the local filesystem, so it gives the same answer on every OS.

## Nx generator templates

This rule is easy to trip over in `tools/generators/*/files/`, because template
contents use EJS. **Put the variable in a file name as `__variable__`, never `<%= variable %>`:**

```text
files/src/pages/__className__Page/__className__Page.tsx   ✅
files/src/pages/<%= className %>Page/<%= className %>Page.tsx   ❌
```

This is not only a Windows concern. Nx's `generateFiles` substitutes `__variable__`
in *paths* and runs EJS over file *contents* — it never evaluates an EJS tag in a file
name. A `<%= className %>` directory is emitted with that literal name on macOS too, so
the generator is broken everywhere; Windows just fails louder and earlier.

Every substitution used in a path must also be passed to `generateFiles`:

```ts
const { name, className } = names(options.name);

generateFiles(tree, joinPathFragments(currentDir, 'files'), `apps/${name}`, {
  name,
  className, // required by __className__ paths *and* by <%= className %> in contents
  displayName,
});
```

A name missing from that object throws `className is not defined` when EJS renders the
template body.
